<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Firozkoh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Firozkoh was the summer capital of the Ghurid dynasty in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a>, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Its exact site was lost after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed it in the 1220s, but it is usually placed in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a>, built by the Ghurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a>. The city was the centre of Ghurid power at its height in the late 12th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Firozkoh means Turquoise Mountain. The city lay in what is now Ghor Province and served the Ghurid dynasty as a summer capital, since the dynasty's leaders were semi-nomadic. Its history, layout and buildings are known chiefly from the Tabaqāt-i Nāsirī of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minhaj-i-siraj-juzjani">Minhaj al-Siraj Juzjani</a>, who lived in the city in his youth but left in 1215 and wrote the work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1260. He is generally thought a reliable chronicler, though his account is one partial view of the city and its people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded in 1146 by Qutb al-Din Muhammad of the Ghurid family. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a> overthrew the Ghaznavid dynasty in 1150 and burned their capital, Ghazna, killing up to 60,000 people; according to Juzjani the surviving citizens were made to carry building materials to Firozkoh as prisoners, and their blood was mixed with mud for building. Under the following sultans the city prospered and rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> as a centre of Ghurid art, literature and theology.</p><p>In 1199 Ghiyath al-Din ordered the empire to give up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karramiyya">Karramiyya</a> sect for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> law, a decision that caused riots in the city. Tombstone inscriptions found in the 1950s, studied by Walter Fischel, point to a Jewish trading community there.</p><p>The empire began to break up after the deaths of Ghiyath al-Din in 1203 and Muizz al-Din in 1206, though Juzjani claimed the treasury still held 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> loads of gold, a figure that may be unreliable. Muhammad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> took the city in 1215, but it rebelled when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> attacked his empire. The Mongols besieged it in 1220 and withdrew at the onset of winter; returning, they forced its ruler, Malik Mubariz al-Din, to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and, according to Juzjani, razed the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site of the city was lost, and it has been proposed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a>, in Shahrak District, is its only standing remains. After the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 2001 hundreds of diggers searched the area for rumoured gold; when Rory Stewart visited in 2002 the possible remains had been heavily damaged, and finds had been sold in the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Firozkoh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghiyath al-Din, the senior sultan, ruled from <b>Firozkoh</b> in Ghor and from Herat and directed the western wars, while Mu'izz al-Din, governing from Ghazni from 1173, led the conquests in India, acknowledging his brother on his coins and in the Friday prayer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
